WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.960
When was this actually proved, Fermat's Last Theorem, by the way?

00:06.920 --> 00:09.200
Yeah, Fermat did not prove it, as far as we know.

00:09.280 --> 00:09.940
He just said it.

00:10.200 --> 00:12.780
Right. This is proved by Andrew Wiles.

00:13.180 --> 00:13.900
Oh, I know that.

00:15.580 --> 00:17.760
And I forget when, but it's in my lifetime.

00:18.120 --> 00:19.240
Yeah, I think it was in the 90s.

00:19.280 --> 00:20.220
In the 90s, right.

00:21.680 --> 00:24.300
Which is why I'm a little bit disappointed in this book.

00:24.300 --> 00:27.980
This book was published in 2001, and it has no discussion of Fermat's Last Theorem,

00:28.060 --> 00:28.940
and it's a number theory book.

00:28.940 --> 00:31.280
How did an editor let that happen?

00:32.720 --> 00:34.460
Yeah, like, I looked. I can't find.

00:34.780 --> 00:36.000
Like, he dances all around it.

00:36.580 --> 00:39.920
Oh, you know why? Andrew Wiles was that he proved it then,

00:39.980 --> 00:42.900
but it wasn't proven until, I think, the other 30 years ago.

00:43.280 --> 00:45.940
So you think the proof was in the...

00:45.940 --> 00:46.880
Nobody took him seriously.

00:47.060 --> 00:48.940
The proof was in the...

00:49.580 --> 00:51.760
I think he was in...

00:51.760 --> 00:55.080
I think Andrew Wiles took the better part of 10 years

00:55.080 --> 00:56.440
to work on this problem in seclusion.

00:56.440 --> 01:01.400
And it took about two years for the proof to be verified.

01:01.660 --> 01:03.560
So, like, I don't think there's an excuse.

01:04.220 --> 01:10.680
Now, the way that Andrew Wiles proved this is very, very complicated.

01:10.680 --> 01:15.840
It involves all of, like, the most sort of high-tech number theory tools.

01:16.020 --> 01:23.340
These somewhere past complex analysis, and it's not complex analysis.

01:23.340 --> 01:24.240
It's much more than that.

01:24.340 --> 01:27.160
It's very cutting-edge mathematics that was required to do this.

01:27.160 --> 01:31.600
And actually, of course, those tools that proved for Maas' last theorem

01:31.600 --> 01:35.700
are now being used to try to open other doors in modern algebraic number theory.

01:37.220 --> 01:38.560
I don't understand those tools.

01:38.660 --> 01:40.100
I just am kind of vaguely aware of them.

01:41.120 --> 01:42.820
And so, yeah.

01:43.340 --> 01:46.380
Now, it turns out that if you...

01:46.380 --> 01:48.460
You can look at other number systems.

01:48.460 --> 01:53.100
In fact, people have thought they proved for Maas' last theorem a while ago.

01:54.020 --> 01:55.740
And I'm trying to find the name here.

01:55.960 --> 01:57.700
It was Lame or Lame.

01:58.280 --> 02:01.100
He found a wrong proof of the for Maas' last theorem

02:01.100 --> 02:04.180
based on adjoining the nth root of unity.

02:05.300 --> 02:07.620
See, like, if you have the nth root of unity,

02:07.620 --> 02:10.740
you can factor that as x minus the nth...

02:10.740 --> 02:11.860
x minus...

02:11.860 --> 02:12.900
How's it go?

02:12.960 --> 02:13.620
x minus...

02:17.620 --> 02:21.360
Well, let me just kind of give you a sense of it.

02:21.760 --> 02:23.380
The reason that the proof...

02:23.380 --> 02:27.380
Here's the algebraic underpinning of Stilwell's argument.

02:27.540 --> 02:28.920
It's this x cubed plus y cubed.

02:29.280 --> 02:31.760
You can write as x minus the third root of unity

02:31.760 --> 02:34.920
times y, I think,

02:35.860 --> 02:36.920
times x...

02:37.620 --> 02:40.580
minus the third root of unity squared times y

02:40.580 --> 02:44.440
times x minus the third root of unity...

02:44.440 --> 02:47.060
No, just x minus y, I believe.

02:48.340 --> 02:49.380
So you just did that out to n?

02:50.720 --> 02:51.080
Yeah.

02:51.220 --> 02:53.000
So if you had the nth root of unity,

02:53.080 --> 02:53.940
you can just keep doing that

02:53.940 --> 02:55.960
and you can factor x to the n plus y to the n.

02:56.440 --> 02:58.600
But the catch is

02:58.600 --> 03:01.420
that adjoining the nth root of unity to the integers

03:01.420 --> 03:03.600
does not give you a unique factorization domain.

03:04.700 --> 03:07.600
And, of course, Lame's arguments would be based on that.

03:08.180 --> 03:09.640
And so that's why it wasn't a proof.

03:10.580 --> 03:12.520
It's exactly this issue that we've faced

03:12.520 --> 03:14.440
with z adjoining the square root of minus three.

03:14.440 --> 03:18.200
Now, Kummer realized that error

03:18.200 --> 03:20.200
and he introduced what are called ideal numbers to fix it.

03:20.820 --> 03:23.260
And then Didakind cleaned up the ideal number concept

03:23.260 --> 03:27.480
and proved the Fermat's last theorem for many choices of n.

03:28.340 --> 03:30.100
